  "worked_solution": "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/a2156d559a715d668f064c91132719d2678ab4af66bf83591fa5687d6e23d95e.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x=2{y}^{2}-2$ and the $y$-axis from $y=-1$ to $y=1.$ \nTo find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2{y}^{2}-2)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{y}^{2}-2)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-4{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}-4\\cdot y{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{3}-0)-4(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}-4 \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So the area that we want is $|-\\frac{8}{3}|=\\frac{8}{3}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
